---
// Sampath › 02 — The plan changed (Figma "sampath-chat-test", node 160:3242): dark text band
// (two columns of paragraphs, a full-width paragraph, then a centred card), then a band of
// two Figma variables screenshots that bleed off its bottom edge.
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import Section from './Section.astro';
import Palette from '../../assets/icons/sampath/palette.svg';
import colours from '../../assets/images/sampath/02_transition-image-replace-with-figma-foundations-__screenshot-2026-09-09-at-22-28-24-1.png';
import typography from '../../assets/images/sampath/03_transition-image-replace-with-figma-foundations-__screenshot-2026-09-09-at-22-31-38-1.png';
---

<Section tone="dark" labelledby="plan-changed-title" tightBottom>
  <h2 id="plan-changed-title">
    <span class="line">The plan changed.</span> <span class="line">The deadline didn't.</span>
  </h2>

  <div class="content">
    <div class="cols text-body-xl">
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          The bank wanted more than fixes. It wanted a branchless app: open accounts, manage cards
          and FDs, and transact by chatting with an AI assistant. And the first release had to go
          live within three months.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our PMs and BAs locked the flows with the client, and the team built the initial flows in
          high-fidelity with Claude as a clickable prototype. It got approval fast.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="col">
        <p>
          Then came the gap. The prototype had unlocked client alignment, but developers could not
          build production UI from it. With the deadline fixed, they needed Figma with
          component-level specs, clear states, and a system they could implement confidently.
        </p>
        <p>
          The plan was for me to build the foundation: a design system in Figma that developers
          could build from, component by component.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="scope text-body-xl">
      Then the scope grew. I'd also design the final UI, using the AI screens as wireframes.
      Onboarding came first, and it was needed within three days.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <div class="point">
        <h3 class="text-title-l">What the AI screens got right</h3>
        <p class="text-body-l body">A direction the client could see and approve, in days.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="point">
        <h3 class="text-title-l">What they missed</h3>
        <p class="text-body-l body">
          I never believed AI screens should be the final UI, and this proved it. Up close in Figma,
          the problems showed. Extra steps users didn't need, a home section title styled unlike
          any other page, and only the happy path, with no edge cases or error states.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p class="note">
        <span class="note-icon" aria-hidden="true"><Palette width={24} height={24} /></span>
        <span class="text-body-m">
          My ownership from here is the Figma design system and all final UI.
        </span>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Section>

<div class="shots" data-dark-hero>
  <!-- On mobile this becomes a swipeable row, so it's focusable for keyboard scrolling. -->
  <div class="canvas" role="region" aria-label="Figma variables screenshots" tabindex="0">
    <Image
      class="shot shot-colours"
      src={colours}
      alt="Figma variables: the Sampath Bank colour collection with light and dark values"
      widths={[800, 1600]}
      sizes="(max-width: 767px) 85vw, (min-width: 1920px) 784px, 41vw"
    />
    <Image
      class="shot shot-type"
      src={typography}
      alt="Figma variables: Sampath Bank alias typography for mobile"
      widths={[810, 1620]}
      sizes="(max-width: 767px) 85vw, (min-width: 1920px) 810px, 42vw"
    />
  </div>
</div>

<style>
  h2 {
    color: var(--text-sampath-secondary);
  }

  /* Two lines from tablet up, as in Figma; on mobile it wraps naturally. */
  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .line {
      display: block;
    }
  }

  /* Figma: 72 below the heading; the blocks are 40 apart and centred. */
  .content {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-40);
    margin-top: var(--space-72);
  }

  /* Two equal columns, 24 apart; paragraphs 28 apart in Figma, snapped to 32. */
  .cols {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    width: 100%;
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  .col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-32);
  }

  .scope {
    width: 100%;
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  /* Figma: 516 wide, padding 24, radius 16, items 24 apart. */
  .card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-24);
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 516px;
    padding: var(--space-24);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background-color: var(--sampath-card-dark-subtle);
  }

  .point {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space-8);
  }

  h3 {
    color: var(--text-sampath-secondary);
  }

  .body {
    color: var(--text-light);
  }

  /* Figma: padding 20/16 and radius 10, snapped to 24/16 and rounded.sm. */
  .note {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-12);
    padding: var(--space-16) var(--space-24);
    border: 0.5px solid var(--sampath-note-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--sampath-note-bg);
    color: var(--text-white);
  }

  /* Figma radius 5, snapped to rounded.sm. */
  .note-icon {
    display: grid;
    flex: none;
    place-items: center;
    width: 44px;
    height: 44px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    background-color: var(--sampath-note-icon-bg);
  }

  /* Mobile: columns stack (paragraph gaps as on desktop); 48 below the heading. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .content {
      margin-top: var(--space-48);
    }

    .cols {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--space-32);
    }
  }

  /* ── Screenshot band ───────────────────────────────────────────────
     A 1920 × 523 Figma canvas, scaled with the viewport. Positions are the Figma
     frame values as percentages: the shots start 41px down and run off the bottom.
     Shots keep their own proportions; only the band's bottom edge cuts them. */

  .shots {
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: var(--sampath-bg-darker);
  }

  .canvas {
    position: relative;
    max-width: var(--width-1920);
    margin-inline: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 1920 / 523;
  }

  .shot {
    position: absolute;
    top: calc(41 / 523 * 100%);
    height: auto;
  }

  /* At x 132, 749 wide (Figma crops its left edge; shown uncropped at the user's request). */
  .shot-colours {
    left: calc(132 / 1920 * 100%);
    width: calc(749 / 1920 * 100%);
  }

  /* At x 978, 810 wide. */
  .shot-type {
    left: calc(978 / 1920 * 100%);
    width: calc(810 / 1920 * 100%);
  }

  /* Mobile: a swipeable row instead of the scaled canvas (confirmed by the user).
     Each shot is 85% wide so the next one peeks in. The row is exactly as tall as the
     first shot (2222 × 1600), so it ends flush with the band; the taller second shot
     runs off the bottom, as on desktop. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .shots {
      container-type: inline-size;
    }

    .canvas {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-start;
      gap: var(--space-16);
      aspect-ratio: auto;
      height: calc(var(--space-32) + (100cqw - 2 * var(--gutter)) * 0.85 * 1600 / 2222);
      padding: var(--space-32) var(--gutter) 0;
      overflow-x: auto;
      overflow-y: hidden;
      scroll-snap-type: x mandatory;
      scroll-padding-inline: var(--gutter);
      scrollbar-width: none;
    }

    .canvas::-webkit-scrollbar {
      display: none;
    }

    .shot {
      position: static;
      flex: none;
      width: 85%;
      scroll-snap-align: start;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Mobile browsers (Safari especially) restore a swiped row's position on reload or
  // when coming back to the page. Always start on the first screenshot.
  const row = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.shots .canvas');
  const reset = () => row?.scrollTo({ left: 0 });

  reset();
  window.addEventListener('pageshow', reset);
</script>
